course: everyday
lesson: 251
title: Younger children, losing and practising
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Little children are still learning how to play: they cry when they lose, wander off at the park and put small things in their mouths, so an older child plays gently and keeps them in sight.
explain: Not winning and not being chosen hurt everyone sometimes; what helps is telling a trusted adult and keeping on practising, because taking part and having fun matter more than trophies.
explain: Skills grow with regular short practice; when something is frustrating, you break it into small steps, and you try a professional's tricky move only slowly, safely and with a coach.
explain: A good team member listens to the coach, asks questions, tries the advice and tells the coach in advance when they will miss practice.
explain: A girl in Vancouver teaches her three-year-old brother to throw: she uses a soft ball and stands close, and soon he is laughing and throwing it back.
fact: Little children are still learning to lose, so you comfort them and play again.
fact: When you look after a younger sibling at the park, you keep them in sight and tell an adult at once if you lose them.
fact: To teach a little one to throw, you use a soft ball and stand close.
fact: Small game pieces can choke a baby, so you move your game to a high table.
fact: Everyone feels left out sometimes; if you are not chosen, tell a teacher or parent and keep practising.
fact: Taking part, fun and practice matter, not only winning.
fact: Skills improve with regular, short practice sessions.
fact: When you get frustrated, you break the skill into small steps.
fact: A professional's move is tried slowly and safely, with a coach, because it may be dangerous.
fact: If you will miss practice, you tell the coach in advance, because the team plans around you.
fact: You listen to the coach, ask questions and try the advice, because coaches help you improve.
exercise: Your little cousin cries because she lost a game of snap. What do you do? | Comfort her and play again | Laugh at her | Tell her to stop playing | Win again to show her how
exercise: Why do little children cry when they lose? | They are still learning how to lose | They are always tired by the end of any game | They just want to annoy you on purpose | They do not like games of any kind and never will
exercise: You are watching your little brother at the park. What must you do? | Keep him in sight | Play on your phone | Go to the shop for a minute | Let him wander off
exercise: You cannot find your little sister at the playground. What do you do? | Tell an adult at once | Keep it a secret | Go home without her | Wait until she comes back
exercise: How do you teach a three-year-old to throw? | Use a soft ball and stand close | Use a hard ball and stand far away so he tries harder | Throw it at him as fast as you can | Use a real cricket ball so it feels proper
exercise: A baby grabs at your board game pieces. What do you do? | Move the game to a high table | Let him play with the small pieces for a while | Hide the pieces under the blanket in his cot | Put the game down on the floor
exercise: Why are small game pieces dangerous for babies? | They can choke on them | They are made of glass | They are too heavy | They are covered in paint
exercise: You were not chosen for a team at break. What can you do? | Tell a teacher or parent and keep practising | Never play any sport again | Pretend you do not care and stay on your own all break | Take the ball home so nobody plays
exercise: Everyone in your league got a trophy. What does it mean? | Taking part matters, not only winning | Nobody really played well | Trophies have no meaning at all | Everybody won every match
exercise: How should you practise a new skill like juggling? | Short sessions, regularly | One very long session | Only before a show | Only when you like
exercise: You keep failing at a backhand in tennis and feel angry. What helps? | Break it into small steps | Give up for good | Throw your racket | Practise only the parts you can do
exercise: You saw a footballer do a bicycle kick on TV. How should you try it? | Slowly and safely, with a coach | On concrete at full speed, just like on TV | Alone in the back garden | Off a high wall so you have time to turn over
exercise: You cannot come to practice on Saturday. What do you do? | Tell the coach in advance | Just not turn up | Tell the coach next month | Ask a friend to pretend to be you
exercise: Your coach shows you a new way to kick. What do you do? | Listen, ask questions and try it | Ignore it and keep doing it your own way | Laugh at the idea and walk away from him | Say that you already know everything about kicking
test: A girl in Wales was picked last for rounders (a bat-and-ball game) again and felt sad. Which is a good step? | Talk to a teacher and keep practising | Stop playing forever | Pick them last next time | Say she hates rounders
test: Why does the team need to know if you will miss practice? | The coach plans the team around you | The coach wants to give you a prize | Practice will be cancelled | It does not matter at all
test: A boy practises piano scales 10 minutes a day; his friend practises once a month for 5 hours. Who will probably get better faster? | The boy who practises daily | The friend who practises for hours | They will be exactly the same | Neither of them, practice does not help
test: You took your little brother to the park and now you cannot see him. What is the right first step? | Tell the nearest adult immediately | Wait an hour to see if he comes back | Go home and say nothing | Keep looking alone and tell nobody
test: Your 2-year-old sister is crawling near your marble game on the floor. What is the danger? | She could put a marble in her mouth and choke | She might win the game | The marbles will get dirty | She could knock all the marbles out of order and spoil the game
test: Learning to skateboard feels hopeless. Which plan is best? | Learn one small step at a time | Try the hardest trick first | Practise only when friends watch | Stop after the first fall
test: A four-year-old loses at tag and cries. Why should an older child be patient? | Little children are still learning to lose | Crying always means that she cheated | She will never play again | It is all the older child's fault for winning
test: A girl tries a high trick from a skating video alone on a steep ramp. Why is this unwise? | It may be dangerous without a coach | Tricks only work on video | Ramps are always closed | It would be too easy
test: lil sis lost at snap n she's crying | Comfort her and play again | Tell her she's a bad loser | Laugh and win again | Stop playing with her forever
test: watching my bro at the park n he's gone?! can't see him | Tell an adult at once | Keep looking alone for an hour | Go home and wait | Climb a tree to look
test: didn't get picked for the team again. so rubbish | Tell a teacher or parent and keep practising | Give up the sport for good | Pick on the kids who were chosen n make them sorry | Pretend you never wanted to play anyway
test: can't miss practice saturday but i've got a party. what do i do | Tell the coach in advance | Just don't turn up | Tell him on Monday | Send a friend instead of you
explain: A girl in Florida was minding her little brother by the pool and looked at her phone; he slipped under the water and only a neighbour's shout saved him.
explain: A boy in Leeds threw his bat after losing a game; it hit a younger child in the face and broke her tooth.
fact: Little children can drown in seconds in very shallow water, so watching them means eyes on them all the time.
fact: Anger at losing can make a child throw things or hit out, and someone smaller is often the one hurt.
test: watching my bro at the pool, gonna check my phone for a sec | Keep your eyes on him the whole time | Fine for a few seconds, nothing happens that fast | Fine if he's in the shallow end | Only if he has his armbands on
test: lost again, wanna throw the bat | No, put it down and cool off | Throw it at the grass, it can't hurt grass | Throw it at the fence | Only throw it gently, so it doesn't break
test: Why must little children never be left alone near water, even shallow water? | They can drown in seconds without a sound | They get bored | They splash too much and soak everyone nearby | The water gets dirty
test: did u hear a girl was looking after her little brother at the park, looked at her phone, and he wandered off to the pond? real lesson? | Keep little ones in sight every moment | It's fine to check your phone briefly | Ponds are safe for toddlers | Little ones always stay put
test: apparently a kid got so mad losing at footy he threw his boot and hit a little kid in the face. what's the true lesson? | Anger at losing can hurt others; calm down first | Throw things only at the big kids, who can take it | Losing is never upsetting | Little kids shouldn't be allowed to watch games
test: my mate says the new kid's useless at sport cos he's never picked first. pass it on? | No, it's unkind; skills grow with practice | Yes, so he stops trying | Yes, it's just honest | Yes, if he's not listening
exam: Identify the most effective pattern of practice for improving a skill. | Regular short sessions | One very long session a year | Practice only before a match | Practice only when one is not tired
exam: Which of the following constitutes appropriate conduct when looking after a younger sibling at a park? | Keeping him in sight and telling an adult at once if lost | Leaving him to play while one plays elsewhere | Searching alone for an hour before telling | Allowing him near water alone
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice when a child will miss a team practice. | He informs the coach in advance | He informs nobody | He informs only a teammate afterwards | He leaves the team
exam: Ascertain the reason for which game pieces are moved to a high table when a baby is present. | Small pieces may choke a baby | Babies may win the game | Pieces are lost more easily on floors | High tables make the game fairer
